# cebuano-directions.kb - Cebuano / Directions
# Format: concept | intent | answer | keywords | patterns | tl | bis
# 4 entries, one genuinely distinct fact per line.
tuyd | definition | Tuyd means to follow or to go after someone, and it is the everyday word for walking behind, following a road, and tracking something. As a verb it is the ordinary word for following a person to a place, as in gitawag nako siya ngadto pa tuyd, and it also covers following a path, so tuyd ang dalan means follow the road. The related sense of following a rule or a leader is carried by the same word, so tuyd kaayo means to do as you are told, and that ambiguity is normal rather than a problem. Tuyd is a transitive verb, so it takes an object: a bare tuyd is heard as follow me, while tuyd ka means follow you and tuyd nato means the two of us follow each other. A learner often reaches for sunod, which is closely related and does mean to follow or next, and the two are used almost interchangeably, with tuyd slightly more common in speech. | follow, after, behind, path, leader, track, transitive | unsa meaning nga tuyd, unsaon nga tuyd, tuyd meaning | sundan | tuyd
imong | definition | Imong is the possessive form of ikaw, and it is the word that fills the gap where English says your or yours, so it is a form rather than a separate word. Its use is narrow and that is the part worth knowing: it applies only to the second person singular, so it is right for one man or one woman and wrong for a group, which takes kamo, and it is never used for the first or third person. Within that, the ending carries the distance: plain imong marks what belongs to or concerns you, the longer form ang imong attaches to a word coming after it, and atong is the respectful version used with someone older or of higher status. Saying imong to a group is a number error rather than a grammar mistake, and dropping the final letter of the word sounds hurried rather than casual. | your, possessive, yours, respectful, second person, form, tone | unsa meaning nga imong, imong meaning, atong vs imong | iyo | imong


ngadto | definition | Ngadto means to a place, in the direction of, and it is the word that marks where an action is going rather than where it is, which is a distinction English usually leaves to a preposition. The paired word is diri, meaning here or to this place, so ngadto and diri together cover going away and coming here. What makes it useful for a learner is that it appears with a place in exactly the position where English would use to: say you went to the market and the Cebuano puts the verb for going and ngadto together, with the place itself unmarked. A related word, diriin, means to or until for a destination, and it is what you use when the place is a person rather than a location, so saying you went to a person rather than to a place takes the other word. A learner who uses ngadto with a person will be understood but will sound as though the person were a location. | there, place, to, destination, direction, away, here | unsa meaning nga ngadto, ngadto meaning, ngadto vs diri | doon | ngadto
